.TH DAVE 8 2026-11-22 "joe Linux" "joe Linux"
.SH NAME
dave \- install, remove and upgrade software on joe Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dave
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ operands ]
.br
.B dave help
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dave
is joe Linux's package manager. It installs and removes packages on the
running system at once, after saving the system as it was as a
.I deployment
that can be booted, so that any change can be undone with
.BR "dave rollback" .
Upgrades are prepared while the system runs and applied as it shuts down.
.PP
What you asked for is kept in
.IR /etc/dave/world ;
everything else installed is there because something you asked for needs it.
dave works out the rest from the signed indexes of the configured
repositories.
.PP
Every command explains itself with
.B dave help
.I command
or
.IR command " " \-\-help .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Packages
.TP
.BI install " package" ...
Install packages and whatever they need. A package may carry a version
constraint, as in
.B hello>=2.0
or
.BR hello=2.0-r1 .
.TP
.BI remove " package" ...
Remove packages you asked for, with the dependencies nothing else needs any
more. A package that is only installed because another needs it cannot be
removed by itself;
.B dave why
says what needs it.
.TP
.B upgrade
Work out an upgrade of every package, download it and prove that it applies
cleanly to a copy of the system. It is applied as the machine shuts down,
and anything changed before then is kept. With
.BR \-\-check ,
only say what an upgrade would change.
.SS Finding out
These need no root. Without it, they use the package index dave fetched last.
.TP
.B list
The installed packages; those installed for another's sake are marked as
dependencies.
.TP
.BI info " package"
The installed and newest available versions, licence, sizes, dependencies,
what depends on it, and why it is installed.
.TP
.BI search " word" ...
The packages whose name or description contains every word, ignoring case.
Names that match come first; installed packages are marked.
.TP
.BI files " package"
The files, directories and links an installed package put on the system.
.TP
.BI owns " path" ...
The installed package a file belongs to. Paths are resolved as the system
resolves them, so
.I /bin/sh
is found although packages install it in
.IR /usr/bin .
.TP
.BI why " package"
Why a package is installed: because you asked for it, or by the shortest
chain of dependencies from something you asked for.
.SS System
.TP
.B status
The running deployment, the one that starts on the next boot, any prepared
upgrade, and every deployment that can be booted.
.TP
.B rollback
Make the deployment before the running one start on the next boot, and on
every boot after it.
.TP
.BR deployments " [" list " | " pin
.IR id " | "
.B unpin
.IR id " | "
.BR gc ]
List the deployments, keep one until it is unpinned, or delete those that
are no longer needed.
.SS Building
.TP
.BI build " recipe"
Build a package from a recipe in a build root
.RB ( \-\-buildroot ", " \-\-sources ", " \-\-work ", " \-\-out ).
.TP
.BI pack " tree"
Make a package of a directory
.RB ( \-\-name ", " \-\-version ", " \-\-out ).
.TP
.BR repo " " index | audit
.I dir
Write and sign a repository's index, or check seals and operation
declarations in the newest release of each package. The audit checks seal
metadata on shared libraries, too. Executable permissions alone do not
make a .so or numerically versioned .so library an undeclared program.
.TP
.BR key " " generate | generate-code
.I basename
Create a key pair for signing indexes, or for sealing programs.
.TP
.B seal
Seal installed files that are not sealed yet; run on an image's first boot.
.SS Images and installation
.TP
.BI "image build" " spec"
Build the disk image, live medium or container image a specification describes.
.TP
.B install-system
With
.BR \-\-list ,
the disks joe Linux could be installed on; with
.BR \-\-disk ,
erase one and install onto it, or with
.B \-\-beside
install into its free space beside the system already there.
.B \-\-enforce
makes the installed system boot with the security module enforcing.
.SS Help
.TP
.BR help " [" \fIcommand\fR ]
Explain dave, or one command and the options it takes.
.TP
.B version
Show dave's version.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS For every command
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
Work on the system installed at
.I dir
instead of the running one.
.TP
.BI \-\-cache " dir"
Keep downloaded indexes and packages in
.IR dir .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Explain the command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show dave's version.
.SS Changing the system
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Go ahead without asking (install, remove).
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done, and stop.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-triggers
Do not run package triggers.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace files that exist but belong to no package.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Only say what an upgrade would change (upgrade).
.TP
.BI \-\-expect " digest"
Make the change only if it is the one
.I digest
names, as a
.B \-\-json
dry run gave it; otherwise change nothing and exit with status 1
(install, remove, upgrade). See
.BR JSON .
.TP
.B \-\-reboot
Apply the prepared upgrade now, and reboot (upgrade).
.TP
.B \-\-requested
Only the packages you asked for (list).
.SS Installing onto a disk
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the disks joe Linux could be installed on.
.TP
.BI \-\-disk " disk"
Erase
.I disk
and install onto it.
.TP
.B \-\-beside
Keep what is on the disk: take its largest free space (22 GB or more) and
share its EFI system partition, so that the boot menu offers the other
system too. Nothing is resized; make the space first, in the other system.
.TP
.B \-\-enforce
Boot the installed system with the security module enforcing
.RB ( joe=enforce ),
so that privileged programs are held to their declarations. Without it the
module only logs what it would refuse.
.TP
.BI \-\-import " dir"
Bring the accounts and homes of the Linux system whose root is mounted at
.I dir
into the new one, with
.BR pour ,
and make those who administered it administrators. It must be on another
disk.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Answer in JSON, for a program: one document for list, info, search,
status, deployments and a look at a change (see
.BR JSON );
for install\-system, progress events, one per line.
.TP
.BI \-\-with " package"
Install
.I package
as well (also for image); install-system takes it from the medium's root,
so it must be installed there.
.SS Building images
.TP
.B \-\-test
Add joe-test-console, which logs root in on the serial console without a
password. For automated tests only.
.TP
.BI \-\-repo " name,dir,url,key"
A repository: read from
.I dir
while building, used as
.I url
by the image, signed by the public key in
.IR key .
.TP
.BI \-\-tree " dir"
Start from
.I dir
instead of the specification's tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " file"
Where the image (or, for build and pack, the package) is written.
.SS Building packages
.TP
.BI \-\-buildroot " dir" "\fR, \fP" \-\-sources " dir" "\fR, \fP" \-\-work " dir"
The build root, the downloaded sources, and where builds happen.
.TP
.BI \-\-driver " file"
The build driver, if not packages/.build/driver.sh.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-work
Keep the work directory of a successful build.
.TP
.BI \-\-code\-key " file"
Seal the package's files with this code-signing key.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " op"
An operation the package declares (pack), or the declaration for recipes
that make none (build).
.TP
.BI \-\-name " name" "\fR, \fP" \-\-version " version" "\fR, \fP" \-\-description " text" "\fR, \fP" \-\-license " spdx"
The package's name, version (with a release, as in 1.0-r1), description
and licence (pack).
.TP
.BI \-\-depends " dep" "\fR, \fP" \-\-conflicts " dep"
A dependency, as in "glibc >=2.44", or a package it cannot be installed
with (pack).
.TP
.BI \-\-key " file"
Sign the index with this secret key (repo index).
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " file"
For repo audit, omit findings already present in a saved audit report.
Save standard output from
.B dave repo audit
as the baseline. Each finding matches by package name, path and reason
across upgrades. Unreadable packages use their filename.
An audit exits with status 1 when findings remain and 0 when none remain.
.SH ASKING FIRST
.B install
and
.B remove
show what they will do. When that is exactly the packages named, they go
ahead; when more would change (dependencies come in, other packages are
upgraded or removed), they ask
.B "Continue? [Y/n]"
first. They only ask when both standard input and standard output are a
terminal;
.B \-\-yes
answers for you. Answering no changes nothing and exits with status 1.
.SH OUTPUT
On a terminal, dave uses colour and redraws one line to show downloads and
progress. Output to a pipe or a file, or to a terminal whose
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ,
is plain lines.
.SH JSON
With
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR list ,
.BR info ,
.BR search ,
.BR status ,
.BR deployments ,
and
.BR install ,
.B remove
or
.B upgrade
with
.B \-\-dry\-run
(and
.BR "upgrade \-\-check" )
print one JSON document on standard output; what is said for people goes
to standard error. Each document has a
.B schema
field,
.BI dave. kind / n\fR.
Fields may be added to a schema without changing
.IR n ;
a field that goes or changes meaning makes it
.IR n +1.
A program should ignore fields it does not know and treat a schema it does
not know as unreadable. A failure prints
.B dave.error/1
with the
.B message
dave says to people and a
.B reason
for the failures a program acts on:
.B changed
(the change is not the one
.B \-\-expect
names; review it again),
.B not_allowed
(see the end of this section) or
.BR needs_root ;
null for any other. It exits with status 1.
.TP
.B dave.list/1
.BR packages :
each
.BR name ,
.BR version ,
.BR requested .
.TP
.B dave.info/1
.BR name ,
.BR description ,
.BR license ,
.B installed
(null, or
.BR version ,
.B reason
(requested, dependency, orphan),
.BR needed_by ,
.BR size ,
.BR files ),
.B available
(null, or
.BR version ,
.BR repository ,
.BR download_size ,
.BR installed_size ),
.BR depends ,
.BR required_by .
.TP
.B dave.search/1
.B index
(fresh, cached, or none when no index has been fetched) and
.BR packages ,
each
.BR name ,
.BR version ,
.BR description ,
.B installed
(null or the installed version). No match is an empty list.
.TP
.B dave.plan/1
What a change would do:
.B operation
(install, remove, upgrade),
.BR index ,
.BR digest ,
.BR install ,
.B upgrade
and
.B remove
(each
.BR name ,
.BR version ,
.B from
for an upgrade,
.BR named ,
.BR requested ),
.BR download_size ,
.B disk_change
(null when a size is unknown) and, for an upgrade,
.B prepared
(null, or the upgrade already waiting for shutdown). No change: empty lists
and a null
.BR digest .
.TP
.B dave.result/1
After a change:
.BR operation ,
.BR digest ,
.B rollback_point
(the deployment
.B dave rollback
returns to).
.TP
.B dave.status/1
.B revision
(changes whenever anything else here does: poll it),
.B managed
(false for a system not made of deployments),
.B complete
(false when only the running deployment could be seen, without root before
dave\-status.service ran at boot),
.BR running ,
.BR next_boot ,
.B deployments
(each
.BR id ,
.BR running ,
.BR next_boot ,
.B health
(good, untested, failed, or null without a boot entry),
.BR pinned ,
.BR reason ,
.BR created ,
.BR parent ),
.B prepared_upgrade
(null, or
.B id
(a new one for each different upgrade prepared),
.BR summary ,
.B install
and
.B remove
counts) and
.B last_upgrade
(null, or
.BR event ,
.BR outcome ,
.BR deployment ,
.BR message ).
.B outcome
is read from the boot loader's counting of attempts:
.B succeeded
(the upgrade runs and passed its boot check),
.B checking
(it runs, its check has not passed yet),
.B fallback
(it failed its check on every attempt and the system before it came back by
itself),
.B not_running
(another deployment was chosen, at the boot menu or with
.BR "dave rollback" ;
nothing failed),
.B not_applied
(the upgrade could not be applied at shutdown;
.B message
says why) or
.BR unknown .
.B event
names the upgrade, so that it can be reported once.
.TP
.B dave.deployments/1
.BR running ,
.BR complete ,
.BR deployments .
.PP
The
.B digest
names a change by its packages and versions. To make exactly the change a
person reviewed, a program shows the
.B dave.plan
of a dry run and then runs the same command with
.B \-\-expect
.IR digest ;
if the repositories or the system have moved meanwhile, nothing is changed.
.PP
A desktop program makes the change through
.BR pkexec (1),
under the polkit action
.B io.joelinux.dave.change
(an administrator's password, remembered for a few minutes). Started by
pkexec, dave installs, removes or upgrades this system's packages and does
nothing else: it requires
.BR \-\-expect ,
and refuses any other command (except
.B install\-system
on the live medium, which its installer runs this way),
.BR \-\-root ,
.BR \-\-cache ,
.BR \-\-no\-triggers ,
.B \-\-overwrite
and
.BR \-\-reboot .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colour.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
When set and not 0, colour even when the output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
means no colour and no redrawn lines.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dave/world
The packages you asked for.
.TP
.I /etc/dave/repos.zon
The repositories, by name and URL.
.TP
.I /etc/dave/keys/
The public keys repository indexes must be signed with.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/dave/db/
What is installed, inside each deployment.
.TP
.I /var/cache/dave/
Downloaded indexes and packages.
.TP
.I /run/dave/status.zon
What
.B dave status
shows, written by root for everyone.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when the command failed, nothing matched, or a question was
answered no.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dave search image viewer
Find a package.
.TP
.B doas dave install loupe
Install it now.
.TP
.B dave why zlib
Find out why something is installed.
.TP
.B dave upgrade \-\-check
See whether there is anything to upgrade.
.TP
.B doas dave upgrade \-\-reboot
Prepare the upgrade and reboot into it.
.TP
.B doas dave rollback
Go back to the system as it was before the last change, on the next boot.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd-boot (7),
.BR btrfs (8)
